# Command Center — read this first, every run

This is a React + TypeScript SPA whose database is Monday.com: the boards ARE the
database, and patients move between stages through Monday automations, not the app.
Each rule below exists because something shipped wrong once.

## Read order
1. `CLAUDE.md` at the repo root. Short on purpose; every AI session loads it. §1 is the
   mental model, §5 the five core mechanisms, §9 the rules that apply everywhere.
2. Grep `docs/claude/11-where-to-look-first.md` for the symptom. It maps about 240
   symptoms to the section that explains them.
3. The `docs/claude/<number>-*.md` file for the area you are in. A code comment citing
   "§5.31d" means that file. They hold the decisions, the measurements taken on the live
   boards, and the ⚠️ notes about what not to do.
4. Before reporting a bug: `docs/claude/10-known-risks.md`, `WRITE_RELIABILITY_AUDIT.md`,
   and `git log --since="7 days ago"`. Of the 14 items in the 2026-09-28 handoff, three
   were already on record and one had been root-caused and fixed the day before.
5. `CURSOR_HANDOFF_REVIEW_2026-09-28.md`: Claude Code's item-by-item response to that
   handoff — what held up, what was stale, how each item was verified, and what an
   independent pass found. Its last section is the long form of this file.

## Mechanisms you must understand before judging a write path
- **Verified write** (`src/lib/shared/verifiedWrite.ts`, CLAUDE.md §5.2): data columns are
  written, read back until Monday returns them, and only then is the stage advancer
  written. An advancer written outside this path, or before the data, is a bug by definition.
- **Automations fire on a status CHANGE, not its value.** Writing the value a column
  already holds returns 200, logs nothing and moves nothing. `expectedText` on an advancer
  task lets `src/lib/shared/advancerNoop.ts` refuse that write. "Press it again" is never a
  fix, and clearing an advancer to "repair" a patient creates a duplicate downstream item.
- **The optimistic hide is a claim with an expiry** (`src/lib/shared/pendingAdvance.ts`).
  Read its header before touching it: the 15 minutes is measured, and "never spend a
  marker on absence" is deliberate.
- **HTTP 200 is not success.** Wrong-shaped values, label ids the column doesn't have, and
  long-text past 2,000 characters all return 200 and write nothing. Status label ids are
  per column and Monday assigns them; read them back from `settings_str`, never infer.
- **Column IDs are the contract** (`src/lib/<role>/mondayMapping.ts`), never titles.
  A blank cell means unknown, not "no".
- **Monday dates are Eastern with no time zone.** Compare ET date parts, never
  `new Date()` in a UTC runtime.
- **Counting contract** (§5.8): `src/hooks/useRoleCounts.ts`, each role's sidebar,
  `scripts/snapshot-baseline.mjs` and `services/baseline-cron/index.mjs` must agree by
  hand. Every "change it in all N places" list in the docs is real.
- **One RingCentral extension for the whole company** (§5.13b,
  `INCIDENT_2026-08-20_RINGCENTRAL.md`): per-patient RingCentral reads happen when a
  patient is opened, never on render or a timer. The call log cannot say who dialed;
  `reportDial` exists for that.
- **Name the screen, not the data** (§5.30): `/welcome-call` and `/care-coordinator`
  share components, so a change to one lands on both.
- **Deep links inject from any group.** Every `src/hooks/*/useMondayPatients.ts` injects a
  `?patientId=` item whatever group it sits in. `?completedStage=` read through
  `useCompletedStageReview` is the write gate for finished records; a page without it is
  writable on a Completed item.

## Hard rules
- **NEVER run the "Sync from Test Repo" GitHub workflow**, by any means (Actions tab,
  CLI, MCP). Only Josh does. It force-pushes test's `main` over prod and running it again
  does not undo it.
- **Typecheck gate is `npx tsc -b --force`.** Never `tsc --noEmit` (it checks zero files
  here). Tests: `npm test`. Many rules are pinned by named tests; if one fails after your
  change, read the section it cites before touching the test.
- **Patient data is on every board.** No phone numbers, DOBs, addresses or full names in
  commits, docs or logs.
- **Durable knowledge goes in `docs/claude/`** as a new numbered file plus one index line
  in `CLAUDE.md` §5. Never bulk up `CLAUDE.md`; never `@`-import a docs file into it.
- **Git:** Josh's standing instruction to Claude Code is push to `main` after rebasing onto
  `origin/main` (baseline-cron and access-config commits land on their own). A dated
  handoff on a branch is fine for a one-off review.

## How to hand off so it gets acted on in the morning
- Situation → What's wrong → Where → Why → Verify, per item. Keep this shape; it worked.
- Name the commit you reviewed, and prefer symbol names to line numbers (lines drift daily).
- Mark each item **Confirmed** (traced end to end) or **Suspected** (plausible; say exactly
  what you could not check).
- Rank by what happens to a patient or a rep: moved to the wrong board > rep re-presses >
  wrong label on a historical row.
- Cross-reference what is already known: "M6, unchanged", "known §10", "fixed in <sha>;
  residual is …".
- Don't include fixes you couldn't run the gates on.
